insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 1, '`knnClassify` trains on the 150 rows of EKENE-1 to EKENE-5 and predicts the first row of EKENE-6 at k 5. What decides the facies it prints for that row?', '["Whichever k-means centre, fitted to the 150 training rows at k 4, lies nearest", "The facies most common among its five nearest training rows, by Euclidean distance on the scaled logs", "Each facies'' mean logs, and the facies whose mean sits closest in raw units", "A weighted vote in which each of the five neighbours counts in inverse proportion to its distance in gAPI"]'::jsonb, 1, 'kNN gives a new row the facies most common among its k nearest training rows, and every distance is Euclidean on the logs scaled with the training rows'' scaler. A k-means centre is a cluster, a numbered group with no rock name, and kNN fits none. Comparing a row with each facies'' mean logs is a nearest-centroid rule, which the engine does not build. Each neighbour casts one vote; nothing is weighted by distance, and no distance here is in gAPI.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 2, 'The engine''s basis for choosing neighbours reads: the k nearest training rows, "taken one at a time". What happens at each of the k steps?', '["All rows are sorted once by raw distance and the first k are kept, whatever their row numbers happen to be", "The row with the smallest squared distance is taken, and a tie is settled by the facies that sorts first", "One row is drawn at random from those within 1.00e-12 of the smallest distance, with a stated seed", "Among rows not yet taken, the lowest row within 1.00e-12, relative, of the smallest squared distance is taken"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis: "the k nearest training rows, taken one at a time: the lowest row among those whose squared distance is within 1e-12 (relative) of the smallest remaining". Distances are on the scaled logs, so a raw sort is a different method. A tie among distances goes to the lower row; the sort order of facies names settles a tied leaf in a tree, a different rule. No random draw enters kNN at all.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 3, 'With EKENE-6 held out, the training scaler centres GR at 54.556667 gAPI, while all 180 cored rows would centre it at 56.871111. Why does the engine use the first figure?', '["The second includes EKENE-6, which would then shape the space it is scored in", "Only the first is a sample mean, divisor n - 1, which the correlation PCA also uses for its centre", "Because the second is refused whenever a held-out well is named, since the engine checks the split", "A smaller centre in gAPI would give shorter scaled distances for every one of the rows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis: "standard scaler (ml.js) fitted on the TRAINING rows only and applied unchanged to the new rows". A scaler fitted on all 180 cored rows lets the held-out well move its own centre and scale, and the score would stop describing an unseen well. A mean has no divisor choice; the sample and population choice is about the standard deviation. The engine does not split wells or check a split, and a smaller centre does not shorten every distance.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 4, 'The same k 5 and held-out EKENE-6 score 0.833333 with the training scaler and 0.466667 when EKENE-6 is standardised on its own statistics. What does the second scaling do to the well?', '["A refusal, since a well scaled on itself counts as a constant log", "Every log is divided by the sample SD, n - 1, lengthening distances", "It maps EKENE-6''s own mean to 0, the training centre, whatever rock the well holds", "It widens EKENE-6''s range to match the 150 training rows, so its rows spread further apart"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Scaled on its own statistics, a well has its own mean at 0, which is where the training rows'' centre sits, so a well rich in one facies is pulled onto the middle of the training mixture and meets the wrong neighbours. Neither wrong way is refused; both return a prediction for every row. Standard scaling here uses the population standard deviation, divisor n. Nothing stretches the well to the training range.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 5, 'With no scaling at all, k 5 on held-out EKENE-6 scores 0.633333. What does the course give as the reason a raw distance behaves as it does?', '["RHOB in g/cm3 differs most between facies and rules every raw distance", "Raw logs leave limestone rows with no neighbours at all, as NPHI and PEF read on different scales", "GR is in gAPI and differs by tens of units between facies, so it rules a raw distance", "Only PEF matters, since it spans more units than the three other logs together, so the raw vote follows it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'GR differs by tens of gAPI between facies while RHOB and NPHI differ by hundredths and PEF by units, so on the raw logs the density, neutron and photoelectric logs barely move a distance. Every row still has neighbours on raw logs; nothing is left without one. RHOB''s spread between facies is in hundredths of a g/cm3, the smallest of the four.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 6, 'Passed GR and a caliper reading 8.5 on all 30 rows of EKENE-1 as training rows, `knnClassify` refuses. Which words does its message use that no clustering function''s message uses?', '["\"on the 30 training rows\", because kNN fits its scaler on the labelled training rows", "\"on the 30 rows passed\", because every scaler in the engine counts the rows passed to it", "\"has zero range\", because kNN scales by the minimum and maximum of each training log", "\"fill or drop missing values first\", because a constant log is read as a missing one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'kNN''s message reads, in the engine''s words: "X.CALI has zero variance on the 30 training rows (every value is 8.5): standardising would divide by zero, so drop the feature or fit on rows where it varies". pca, kmeans, silhouette, elbow and agglomerative fit on every row passed and say "on the 30 rows passed". "zero range" belongs to the min-max scaler, and a constant log is a value the scaler cannot divide by, which is a different refusal from a missing one.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 7, 'Trained on all 180 cored rows at k 2, row 27 of EKENE-8 has two neighbours, shaly-sand at 0.372564 and sandstone at 0.379644 standard units. What does the engine predict, and what would scikit-learn print?', '["Both print sandstone, the facies that sorts first, since the two tie rules agree on this row", "Neither prints a facies, because a tied vote at an even k is refused until k is made odd", "The engine predicts sandstone because a tie falls to the lower row; scikit-learn prints shaly-sand", "The engine predicts shaly-sand, the nearer neighbour''s facies; scikit-learn would print sandstone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis: "a tied vote goes to the tied label whose nearest member comes first in the neighbour order (scikit-learn takes the label that sorts first)". The nearer neighbour is shaly-sand, so the engine prints shaly-sand; sandstone sorts before shaly-sand, so scikit-learn would print sandstone. A tied vote is a result: `tiedVotes` counts 1 such row in the well. The lower-row rule settles equidistant neighbours, a different tie.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 8, 'The golden `knn-vote-tie-nearest-b` puts training rows at 0, 1, 3, 4 and 10, labelled b, a, a, b, c, and a new row at 0.2 with k 4 and no scaling. The votes are a 2 and b 2. What does the engine predict?', '["a, the facies that sorts first among the two tied labels", "b, the facies of the nearest neighbour, the row at 0", "c, since the row at 10 breaks a tie between the other two", "nothing: a two-way tie at k 4 is refused by name as `k`"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The neighbours, nearest first, are the rows at 0, 1, 3 and 4 (rows 0, 1, 2, 3, counted from 0), labelled b, a, a, b. The tied facies whose nearest member comes first in the neighbour order is b, from the row at 0. A rule taking the label that sorts first would give a. The row at 10 is not among the four neighbours, so it casts no vote, and a tied vote is a result with a prediction.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 9, 'Why can an odd k still meet a tied vote on the Ekene wells?', '["Four facies can split the votes, so at k 5 votes of 2, 2 and 1 tie two facies", "Odd k counts the new row as its own neighbour, leaving an even vote", "Because the tie band of 1.00e-12 makes some neighbours count as half a vote each at odd k", "Each cored well adds one neighbour before voting, so the count of votes turns even"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An odd k rules out a tie only between two classes. The course has four facies, so votes of 2, 2 and 1 at k 5 leave two facies level. The new row is never among the training rows it is compared with. The tie band decides which rows are neighbours, and each neighbour then casts one whole vote. Nothing picks neighbours by well.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 10, 'In the golden `knn-equidistant-lower-row`, training rows sit at 0, 2, 4 and 9, labelled a, b, c, c; a new row sits at 2, k 2. Rows 0 and 2 are both 2.000000 away. Which rows are the neighbours, and what is predicted?', '["Row 1 together with row 2, predicting c", "A refusal: rows 0 and 2 tie, so no vote is taken", "All three of rows 1, 0 and 2, as tied rows are kept", "Rows 1 and 0, and the prediction is b"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Row 1 sits on the new row and is the first neighbour. Of rows 0 and 2, equally far, the engine takes the lower row, row 0. The neighbours are rows 1 and 0, labelled b and a, a tied vote, which goes to the facies of the nearer neighbour, row 1: b. Taking row 2 would be the higher row. kNN keeps exactly k neighbours, and a tie in distance is settled, never refused.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 11, 'Two squared distances to a new row are equal on paper but differ in their last bits once computed. How does the engine decide whether they tie?', '["Only when the two figures agree at six decimals, the precision the course prints distances at", "They tie when within 1.00e-12 of the smallest remaining squared distance, relative to it, inclusive", "Within 1.00e-9 of each other, relative to the larger one, the same band the sign rule uses", "Never: a bit-level difference orders them, and the smaller always becomes the neighbour"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine judges equally far inside a band: a squared distance within 1.00e-12 of the smallest remaining, relative to it, is tied, and the band is inclusive; the lower row then wins. Six decimals hide differences far larger than that band, so printing alike is not the test. 1.00e-9 is the sign rule''s band for a PCA weight, a different rule. Ordering by the last bits is what the band exists to avoid.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 12, 'Held-out EKENE-6 scores 0.833333 at k 1, 0.866667 at k 3, 0.833333 at k 5 and 0.766667 at k 15. How does the course quote the choice of k?', '["Keep k 1, because it ties the default k 5 at 0.833333 and it uses the fewest neighbours of all", "Choose k 15, as a larger k averages over more rows and so generalises best of all", "k 3 scored highest on this one well, and the k is quoted with the well it was chosen on", "For the whole Ekene field k 3 is best, since three is the smallest odd k that avoids ties"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The highest accuracy on this one held-out well is 0.866667 at k 3, and one held-out well is one draw: another well could rank k differently, so the k is quoted with its well. Scoring every well in turn is cross-validation, which is the machine learning course''s. k 15 scored lowest in the table. An odd k does not avoid ties among four facies, and two figures printing 0.833333 say nothing about which k to keep.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 13, 'Trained on all 180 cored rows, `knnClassify` is asked for more neighbours than it has. Which k values does its boundary accept?', '["k 1 to 179 are accepted, and 180 is refused", "Any odd k up to 180 is accepted, even k refused", "k 0 to 180 are accepted, 0 meaning every row", "k 1 and k 180 are accepted; one more is refused"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s words for the refusal: "k must be a whole number from 1 to 180 (the training rows)". The boundary table draws it for this rule: 1 and the number of training rows accepted, one more refused. Even k is accepted; the engine resolves tied votes by its stated rule. k 0 is outside "from 1".'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 14, 'With k equal to the number of training rows, every new row votes with every training row. What does its vote then depend on?', '["Only the facies counts of the training rows, the same for every new row, unless those counts tie", "Only its own nearest row, since the tie rule falls back to the single nearest training row", "The distances to every training row, each vote weighted by how near that training row sits", "The share of each facies among the new rows, which the engine counts before any vote is cast"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When every training row is a neighbour, the votes are the facies counts of the training rows, the same for every new row; only a tie in those counts would bring the row''s own neighbour order back in through the tie rule. The engine accepts that k, and the held-out score says whether it is useful. No vote is weighted, and the new rows'' own facies are unknown.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-k-nearest-neighbours', 15, '10000 new rows against 10001 training rows are refused with "100010000 distance pairs, above the 100000000 kNN computes". What does the course draw from the cap?', '["A cap on training rows alone, so 10000 is the most kNN can train on", "kNN measures every training row against every new row, so a long section is classified in batches", "Above the cap the engine keeps a seeded sample of 10000 new rows and warns that the rest were dropped", "The pair count is squared rows, so thinning the new rows helps and thinning the training rows cannot"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The work grows with the training rows times the new rows, and the cap is on that product: 10000 x 10000 = 100000000 is computed and 10001 x 10000 is refused. The message names its remedy: "classify fewer rows at a time or thin the training rows". Nothing is sampled or dropped silently; the engine refuses. Either factor reduces the product.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 1, 'The root of the tree holds the 180 cored rows, 54, 50, 29 and 47 of limestone, sandstone, shale and shaly-sand. What is its Gini impurity, and how is it formed?', '["0.283413: the drop the root split achieves on those rows", "0.300000: the share of the largest facies, limestone, 54 of 180", "0.738704: 1 less the sum of the squared share of each facies", "0.650416: the impurity left once the limestone rows are split off"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gini impurity is 1 - sum p_c^2, and the engine returns 0.738704 for the root, matching 1 - ((54/180)^2 + (50/180)^2 + (29/180)^2 + (47/180)^2). 0.283413 is the decrease of the root''s split, a property of a split and never of a node. 0.300000 is the training accuracy of a single leaf predicting limestone, on the rows it was grown on. 0.650416 is the Gini of node 2, the 126 rows the root sends right.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 2, 'The root splits on NPHI at 0.123000 with a weighted impurity decrease of 0.283413. Its right child, node 2, holds 126 rows with Gini 0.650416. What is the Gini of the left child?', '["0, because it holds the 54 limestone rows and nothing else", "0.283413, the decrease the split achieved, carried by the left side", "0.499522, the Gini the engine prints for the next split node down", "0.300000, the share of the rows the split sends to the left"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The root split sends exactly the 54 limestone rows left, and a node of one facies has impurity 0. The decrease is a property of the split, measured against the root: 0.738704 less the rows-weighted impurity of the two children, (126/180) x 0.650416. Subtracting the two Gini figures unweighted gives no child''s impurity, and a share of rows is not an impurity.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 3, 'Node 2 splits its 126 rows on GR at 95.600000 with a weighted impurity decrease of 0.186104, while node 10, deep in the tree, splits 16 rows with a decrease of 0.031566. Why is node 2''s figure so much larger?', '["GR is in gAPI, the log with the largest unit, and a decrease is measured in the unit of its log", "Deeper nodes subtract the impurity of every node above them, so their decreases shrink by design", "Node 10 is split on RHOB, and any split on density is scaled down by the sample SD before scoring", "The decrease is weighted by each node''s share of all 180 rows, so a split near the root counts for more"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A node''s impurity enters the decrease weighted by its rows over the whole tree''s rows, so a split that moves many rows near the root can show a larger decrease than a cleaner split deep down. Gini is computed on facies counts and has no log unit, and no scaling enters a tree. A node''s decrease compares it with its own two children only.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 4, 'Within a node, where may the engine place a candidate threshold on a log?', '["Every distinct value of the log in the node is a candidate, so a threshold always equals some training value exactly", "At a/2 + b/2 for each pair of consecutive distinct values a and b, or at a when that midpoint rounds to b", "Only at the mean of the log over the node''s rows", "On a grid of quantiles of the log, stepping in tenths"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "midpoints a/2 + b/2 of consecutive distinct values of the node (a when that rounds to b); x <= threshold goes left". Any threshold between two neighbouring values sends the same rows each way, so the midpoint stands for the whole gap. A training value sits on its threshold only in the guarded case, where the threshold is a. A mean or a quantile grid is another method.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 5, 'A new row reads NPHI 0.123000, exactly the root threshold of the five-channel tree. Which way does the root send it?', '["Left, with the limestone rows, because x <= threshold goes left", "Right, because a value on the threshold belongs with the larger side of the gap", "Neither way: the row sits on a boundary and `cartPredict` refuses it by name", "Left or right by the tie band, since 1.00e-12 of the threshold counts as either side"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis draws this boundary for this rule: "x <= threshold goes left", so a value exactly at the threshold goes left, where the root sends the limestone rows. `cartPredict` returns the facies of the leaf every new row reaches; it has no refusal for a value on a threshold. The 1.00e-12 band governs distance and merge-height ties, and a threshold comparison uses none.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 6, 'The printed tree carries the line `|   |   |   |   |--- RHOB <= 2.3810000000000002`, and the node table prints the same threshold as 2.381000. How does the course quote it?', '["Quote 2.3810000000000002 in working, as the longer form is the more exact value of the threshold", "Round the printed line to three decimals and treat the tail as a rounding error the engine should have cleared", "Quote 2.381000, the field at six decimals; use the printed line only verbatim as the engine''s words", "Treat the two as different thresholds, one used to fit the tree and one printed for the learner"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The printed tree shows each threshold as the shortest decimal that reads back to the stored number, so a midpoint that is not exact in binary prints with a tail. Both forms describe one stored threshold. A figure a learner reasons with is the numeric field at the course''s precision; the printed line may appear only verbatim, as the engine''s words. The tail is how binary represents that midpoint, and nothing in the engine is at fault.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 7, 'Grown on all 180 cored rows, the five-channel tree reads a training accuracy of 0.927778 at maxDepth 3, 0.950000 at 4, 0.988889 at 5 and 0.988889 at 6. What is the stated behaviour of that column?', '["Training accuracy rises at every step of depth until every leaf is pure", "Training accuracy peaks at the default depth and falls beyond it on these rows", "Training accuracy tracks the held-out score, rising and falling with it", "Training accuracy never falls as the tree deepens, on the rows it was grown on"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Splitting a leaf never lowers the count of its own rows predicted right, so the training accuracy never falls; it holds at 0.988889 from depth 5 to 6, so it does not rise at every step. Grown without EKENE-6 and scored on it, the held-out accuracy moves up and down: 0.900000 at maxDepth 4, 0.766667 at 5 and 0.900000 at 6. The two columns answer different questions.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 8, 'Trees grown on EKENE-1 to EKENE-5 and scored on EKENE-6, held out, read 0.633333 at maxDepth 3, 0.900000 at 4, 0.766667 at 5 and 0.900000 at 6. What does the course conclude?', '["maxDepth 4 is the best depth for any facies tree, since it is the shallowest one to reach 0.900000", "Held-out accuracy moves up and down with depth, and one held-out well is what says how far a deep tree carries", "The default depth of 5 overfits these wells, so the engine lowers it to 4 whenever a well is held out", "maxDepth 6 is preferred over 4 because at 0.900000 on both it also reaches the higher training accuracy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The held-out accuracy is highest, 0.900000, at maxDepth 4 and 6, and moves with depth; a deep tree fits its own rows, and the held-out well says how far that carries, as one draw. Nothing makes one depth best for every study, and the engine never changes maxDepth: it grows to the default 5 when maxDepth is left out. Preferring a depth by its training accuracy is the reading the held-out column exists to check.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 9, 'With `minSamplesLeaf` 5, stated, the five-channel tree on the 180 cored rows has 8 leaves, the smallest holding 5 rows, and a training accuracy of 0.972222. What does that setting forbid?', '["Any node of fewer than 5 rows from being split again", "Any leaf whose most common facies holds fewer than 5 rows", "Any split that would leave fewer than 5 rows on either side", "Growing more than 5 leaves below any one split node of the tree"]'::jsonb, 2, '`minSamplesLeaf` forbids any split that leaves fewer than it on a side; without it, at the default 1, the smallest leaf holds 2 rows and the training accuracy on the rows it was grown on is 0.988889. Refusing to split a small node is `minSamplesSplit`, a different setting whose default is 2. The setting counts rows on each side of a split; it does not count a facies inside a leaf or cap the leaves.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 10, '`cartFit` is passed maxDepth -1. What comes back?', '["A refusal: \"maxDepth must be a whole number, 0 or more (0 is a single leaf)\"", "One single leaf, since the engine reads any depth below 0 as the smallest one", "The default tree of depth 5, with a warning that the depth given was ignored", "A refusal naming `minSamplesSplit`, as a negative depth leaves no row to split"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The boundary table: maxDepth 0 is accepted and gives a single leaf, and -1 is refused. The refusal names its own field, `maxDepth`, and the engine''s words are quoted in full in the key. A setting outside its range is never replaced by a default and never answered with a warning.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 11, 'In the printed five-channel tree, the sandstone leaf reads `class: sandstone (n = 45, counts 0/44/0/1)`. What do the counts say?', '["Of its 45 rows, 44 were predicted right on EKENE-6, held out, and 1 was predicted wrong", "The leaf sits at depth 0, 44 rows below the root, with 1 row still to split beneath it", "Of its 45 rows, 44 are sandstone and 1 is shale, counted in the order the logs were passed", "Of its 45 rows, 44 are sandstone and 1 is shaly-sand, in the order limestone, sandstone, shale, shaly-sand"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A leaf line names its facies, its rows and each facies'' count in the order the engine sorts them, limestone, sandstone, shale, shaly-sand. The fourth count is shaly-sand, and shale is the third. The tree was grown on all 180 cored rows, so the counts are training rows; EKENE-6 was not held out here. The order of the counts follows the facies names, whatever order the logs came in.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 12, 'Two leaves of the five-channel tree are mixed: 0/44/0/1 of 45 rows and 0/1/0/10 of 11 rows. Every other leaf is pure. What training accuracy does that give, on the rows the tree was grown on?', '["0.950000: the 11-row leaf is scored as a whole miss", "0.988889: 2 of the 180 cored rows are predicted wrong", "0.972222: each mixed leaf costs two rows, one per side", "0.833333: the held-out score of the same tree on EKENE-6"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each mixed leaf holds one row of another facies, 1 shaly-sand in the sandstone leaf and 1 sandstone in the shaly-sand leaf, so all but 2 of the 180 rows are predicted as their core facies, and the engine returns 0.988889. A leaf predicts its most common facies, so its majority rows are right. 0.833333 is the kNN score at k 5 on EKENE-6, held out, a different method on different rows.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 13, 'The engine numbers the nodes of the five-channel tree depth first, left before right. The root is node 0. Which node is the GR split at 95.600000?', '["Node 1, as the first split below the root always takes number 1", "Node 3, as the leaves are numbered only after every split node", "Node 14, as the numbering runs upward, starting from the deepest node", "Node 2, since node 1 is the limestone leaf to the root''s left"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis: "numbered depth first, left before right (scikit-learn tree_ order)". The root''s left child, the limestone leaf, is visited first and is node 1; the right child, the GR split, is node 2, as the node table shows. Leaves take their numbers in that same walk. The shale leaf, reached last, is node 14.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 14, 'Which of these is one of the engine''s stated reasons for making a node a leaf?', '["The best split available lowers the Gini impurity by exactly zero", "Its training accuracy, on the rows it holds, has reached the default target of 0.950000", "All of its rows come from a single well, so any split would only fit that one well", "The node''s Gini is below the default floor of 1.00e-12, relative"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis lists every rule: "a node is a leaf when pure, at depth maxDepth 5 (the root is depth 0), with fewer than minSamplesSplit 2 rows, when no split leaves minSamplesLeaf 1 rows each side, or when the best decrease is zero". There is no accuracy target and no rule about wells. 1.00e-12 is the distance and merge-height tie band; a tree compares decreases exactly on integer counts.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-classification-trees', 15, '`cartFit` is passed the facies as a mix of names and numbers, and refuses with the field `y[2]`. What is its message?', '["\"y[2] must be a string or a finite number\", because a name is not a number", "\"y must be an array of 180 labels, one per row\"", "\"y[2] must be the same type as y[0]: all strings or all numbers\"", "\"names must be an array of 4 feature names, one per column\""]'::jsonb, 2, 'cartFit refuses a mixed label list with the words in the key: every label must share the type of the first. The form "must be a string or a finite number" is the refusal of a null facies, as knnClassify words it for `y[1]`, and the question passed no null. The length refusal and the names refusal are real messages about other faults, with other fields: `y` in knnClassify and `names` in cartFit.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 1, 'Two depth-1 trees are grown on the 180 cored rows. With the logs in the order GR, RHOB, NPHI, PEF, the root is NPHI <= 0.123000; with PEF before NPHI, it is PEF <= 4.090000. Both show a decrease of 0.283413. What decided each root?', '["NPHI''s decrease is larger in its last bits, so it always wins", "The lower threshold wins a tie, and 0.123000 is below 4.090000, so NPHI would win in both orders", "A seeded draw among the tied logs, made from mulberry32", "The engine returns the two decreases exactly equal, and the tie goes to the lower column index"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis: "equal decreases (compared exactly on the integer counts) go to the lower feature index, then the lower threshold (scikit-learn breaks feature ties at random)". The two splits make the same two groups, so the counts are identical and the decreases exactly equal. The feature index comes first; the threshold only breaks a tie within one log. cartFit takes no seed and draws nothing.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 2, 'Why do NPHI and PEF tie for the root of the tree on the Ekene cored rows?', '["A correlation of -0.622647, near enough to 1 in size to tie", "Each separates exactly the 54 limestone rows from the other 126, as the generator planted", "Both carry the same importance in the five-channel tree, and importance decides the root split", "Both logs have their thresholds within 1.00e-12 of each other after standard scaling of the rows"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The generator held limestone NPHI at or below 0.12 and every other facies'' NPHI at or above 0.13, and limestone PEF at or above 4.2 and every other facies'' PEF at or below 3.9. Each log alone sends the same 54 limestone rows one way, so both splits leave identical counts. A correlation or an importance is not what a split is scored on, and a tree scales nothing and uses no band.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 3, 'In the tree whose logs are ordered GR, RHOB, PEF, NPHI, the root is PEF <= 4.090000 and sends 126 rows left. Where do the limestone rows go?', '["Right, as a pure node of 54 rows with Gini 0", "Left, with the other 126 rows, since limestone reads the lowest PEF", "Right, as a node of 126 rows with Gini 0.650416, left to be split again", "Left, as in the NPHI tree, because a column order never moves which side a group takes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Limestone PEF is at or above 4.2, above the threshold 4.090000, so x <= threshold sends the 126 other rows left and the 54 limestone rows right, a node of one facies with Gini 0. In the NPHI tree the limestone goes left, NPHI <= 0.123000, and the right child holds 126 rows with Gini 0.650416. The column order changes the log at the root, the threshold and the side each group takes.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 4, 'Distance ties in kNN, k-means and agglomerative clustering are judged inside a relative band. How is a tie between two candidate splits judged?', '["Inside the same band of 1.00e-12, relative to the larger of the two weighted decreases", "At six decimals, the precision at which the course prints every weighted impurity decrease", "Exactly, on the integer facies counts of the children, so an equal decrease is equal with no band", "Inside a band of 1.00e-9, relative, the same band the engine uses for its PCA sign rule"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis says the decreases are "compared exactly on the integer counts": two splits that send the same rows each way give identical counts, so there is no rounding to hide behind, and a tie here is a tie. The 1.00e-12 band is for distances and merge heights, 1.00e-9 for the PCA sign rule. Six decimals is how the course prints a figure, which decides nothing.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 5, 'What would scikit-learn do on the same tied root, and what does the engine give up by choosing otherwise?', '["Both splits are kept in one node by scikit-learn, so what the engine gives up is a tree with two roots", "scikit-learn breaks feature ties at random; the engine''s column order becomes part of the model", "scikit-learn refuses a tie; the engine gives up the refusal and prints a warning in its place", "It would take the log that sorts first; the engine gives up the alphabetical rule"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conventions table: CART split tie, this engine "lower feature index, then lower threshold, compared exactly", alternative "a random feature order (scikit-learn random_state)", chosen because "the same data give the same tree". The cost is that the order the logs are passed in decides the printed tree, so it is written down with the model. A tie is never refused or warned about, and no tree has two roots.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 6, 'The golden `cart-xor-no-split` has rows (0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1) labelled 0, 1, 1, 0 and maxDepth 3. What tree does the engine return?', '["A depth 2 tree that separates all four labels", "A refusal naming `y`, as the labels are balanced", "A root split on the first feature, then two leaves", "A single leaf, 1 node, predicting 0"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The root holds two rows of each label, Gini 0.500000. Split on either feature and each side still holds one 0 and one 1, so no single split lowers the Gini, and a decrease of exactly zero leaves a leaf. The engine refuses the zero-decrease split, so it never reaches the second level where a split would separate the labels. Balanced labels are a valid input.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 7, 'The labels of the XOR golden are fully decided by the two features together. Why does CART still stop at the root?', '["It judges one split at a time, and the first split alone gains nothing", "Its default maxDepth of 5 is too shallow for two features to be combined", "Its tie band treats the Gini of 0.500000 as pure once it is below 1.00e-12", "It needs minSamplesLeaf 5, and four rows cannot meet that on any side"]'::jsonb, 0, 'CART is greedy: it scores each split on its own, and here every single split leaves each side half one label and half the other, so its decrease is zero and the node stays a leaf. The golden sets maxDepth 3, so depth is not the limit; the default minSamplesLeaf is 1, and a Gini of 0.500000 is far from 0. The label is 1 exactly when the two features differ, a rule only a pair of splits could draw.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 8, 'Which rule for a split with a decrease of exactly zero is the engine''s, and which is scikit-learn''s?', '["Both allow it, but the engine warns that the split separated nothing", "The engine allows it once per branch; scikit-learn never allows it", "The engine refuses it and the node stays a leaf; scikit-learn allows it", "Both refuse it, since a zero decrease cannot be compared with zero"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conventions table: CART zero-decrease split, this engine "refused: the node stays a leaf", alternative "allowed (scikit-learn)", reason "a split that separates nothing is not a split". The boundary table draws it exactly: a decrease above zero splits, and a decrease of exactly zero leaves a leaf. No warning is attached; the rule simply stops the node.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 9, 'The golden `cart-iris-depth0` grows a tree of maxDepth 0 on Fisher''s iris: one leaf holding 50 setosa, 50 versicolor and 50 virginica flowers. What does it predict, and what is its training accuracy on the rows it was grown on?', '["virginica, the class listed last; 0.333333", "no class, since a tied leaf is refused", "setosa, since it is the most common class; 1.000000", "setosa, the class that sorts first; 0.333333"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis for a leaf: "the majority class of the leaf; a tie goes to the class that sorts first". setosa sorts first among the three tied classes, so the leaf predicts setosa, and every setosa flower is right and every other wrong: 50 of 150, 0.333333. No class is more common here; the three tie. A tied leaf still names a class.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 10, 'A leaf of the five-channel tree could hold equal counts of sandstone and shaly-sand rows. Which facies would it predict?', '["Sandstone, the name that sorts first by character", "Shaly-sand, since the tree reads the facies of the nearest training row", "Whichever of the two has the lower mean GR over the rows in the leaf", "Neither: the leaf is split again until its counts are no longer tied"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A tied leaf goes to the class that sorts first, and names sort by character, so sandstone comes before shaly-sand, as shale does. The rule reads the spelling of the names, whatever the logs look like. A tree keeps no neighbour order and no log means at its leaves, and a leaf is a leaf because a stopping rule was met, so it is never split again to break a tie.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 11, 'The same engine settles a tied kNN vote and a tied tree leaf by different rules. Which pairing is right?', '["kNN: the class that sorts first; tree: the facies of the row nearest the leaf''s centre", "kNN: the tied facies whose nearest member comes first; tree: the class that sorts first", "Both use the class that sorts first, which is the one tie rule the engine uses everywhere", "kNN: the lower training row; tree: the lower column index of the log split at the leaf"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each rule is stated where it applies and none is global. kNN''s basis: "a tied vote goes to the tied label whose nearest member comes first in the neighbour order". The tree''s: "a tie goes to the class that sorts first", because a leaf keeps no neighbour order. The lower training row settles equidistant neighbours, and the lower column index settles tied splits: both are rules for other ties.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 12, 'The five-channel tree reports importances GR 0.282009, RHOB 0.244767, NPHI 0.444883, PEF 0.028341 and CALI 0.000000. How is an importance formed?', '["The drop in held-out accuracy on EKENE-6 when that log is shuffled with a seed and the tree rescored", "Count the splits made on each log, then divide by the 7 splits of the tree so the shares sum to 1", "Per log, the sum over its splits of n_t gini_t - n_L gini_L - n_R gini_R, normalised to sum 1", "Each log''s absolute weight on the first principal component, normalised to sum 1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis: "sum over the feature''s splits of n_t gini_t - n_L gini_L - n_R gini_R, normalised to sum 1 (scikit-learn feature_importances_); all 0 for a single leaf". It is the impurity each split removed, counted in rows. A shuffled-log score is permutation importance, the machine learning course''s method. Counting splits would give GR, RHOB and NPHI, two splits each, one and the same share, and a PCA weight is another quantity.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 13, 'PEF separates the limestone rows exactly, as NPHI does, yet its importance in the five-channel tree is only 0.028341. Why?', '["It lost the root to NPHI on the column-index tie-break, and its one split sits deep among 47 rows", "Its values are in b/e, a smaller unit than NPHI''s v/v, and importances are measured in log units", "Once scaled by the population SD its spread is the narrowest of the four logs on the cored rows", "Its separation of limestone is only approximate, and CART discounts any split that leaves a miss"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With NPHI before PEF, NPHI takes the root, and once the limestone rows have gone left, PEF''s limestone split is already made. PEF''s only split is at node 5, among 47 sandstone and shaly-sand rows. Importances are computed on Gini counts, with no unit and no scaling, and PEF''s limestone split is exact: the planted bounds hold on every row.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 14, 'In the two depth-1 trees of the root tie, what importance does each log take?', '["NPHI and PEF share it equally in both orders, since their decreases tie exactly", "Zero for both, as a depth-1 tree has no split below its root", "The log listed first in the order takes 1.000000, whichever log that happens to be", "NPHI takes all of it, 1.000000, in one order and PEF all of it in the other"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A depth-1 tree has one split, so the log at the root takes the whole normalised importance: NPHI 1.000000 in the order GR, RHOB, NPHI, PEF and PEF 1.000000 in the order GR, RHOB, PEF, NPHI. The two logs split the rows the same way, and each in turn is reported as the whole story. Importances are all 0 only for a single leaf. GR is listed first in both orders and takes none.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-ties-and-exact-comparisons', 15, 'What does an importance of 0.000000 for CALI in the five-channel tree tell a reader?', '["The scaler refused it as a constant log and dropped it silently", "The tree never split on CALI, which matches the planted caliper that carries no facies signal", "Any caliper, in any well of any field, carries no facies information and should be left out of study", "It tied with another log at every split and lost on column index"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CALI is never split on, and its importance is 0.000000; the generator drew it independently of the facies and of every other log. A tree scales nothing, so no scaler refusal arises, and a refused log would stop the call by name. An importance describes this one tree and says nothing universal about calipers. CALI is the fifth column, the highest index, but nothing shows it tied for any split.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 1, 'To predict EKENE-7 and EKENE-8, what does the course train each method on?', '["All 180 cored rows of EKENE-1 to EKENE-6: kNN at k 5, and the five-channel tree at the default depth", "The 150 rows of EKENE-1 to EKENE-5, keeping EKENE-6 held out so each prediction still carries a score", "All 240 rows of the eight wells, with the uncored rows given the facies of their nearest k-means cluster", "The 60 uncored rows, each well scaled on its own statistics"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For prediction both methods train on all 180 cored rows: kNN with k 5, and the tree of GR, RHOB, NPHI, PEF and CALI at the default depth. The held-out scores on EKENE-6 came from separate runs and are the evidence carried with the prediction. An uncored row has no facies to learn from, and a cluster number is no facies. Scaling a well on its own statistics is the wrong way that scored 0.466667 on EKENE-6.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 2, 'A training set that still holds EKENE-7''s rows, whose FACIES entries are null, is passed to `knnClassify`. What comes back?', '["Predictions for the new rows, with the null rows dropped from the training set and a warning", "Null rows voting as a facies of their own", "A refusal naming the first null label, such as \"y[1] must be a string or a finite number\"", "A refusal telling the caller to fill the missing facies with the nearest cored row''s facies first"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses a null facies by name and neither guesses a facies for it nor drops the row; keeping uncored rows out of the training set is the caller''s step. A dropped-row warning or a null facies voting would both be the engine deciding something it leaves to the caller. The only fill-or-drop message in the engine concerns a missing value in X, and even there it fills nothing.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 3, 'Trained on the cored wells, kNN at k 5 predicts EKENE-8 as 13 limestone, 2 sandstone, 12 shale and 3 shaly-sand rows. What does that count, on its own, establish?', '["EKENE-8 holds more shale than EKENE-7, whose kNN count shows only 1 shale row of its 30", "Nothing about whether the predictions are right; it is a count of predicted facies", "The tree is wrong on EKENE-8, since it predicts 3 sandstone rows where kNN predicts only 2", "kNN reads EKENE-8 at an accuracy of 0.833333, the held-out score it carries over to the well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing in the prediction counts says whether either well is predicted right. The shale counts compare predictions, and whether EKENE-8 holds more shale is a claim about rock that core would settle. A disagreement between methods shows at least one is wrong on those rows, and says nothing about which. 0.833333 is kNN''s score on EKENE-6, held out, the expected agreement written back with the prediction, and it is no accuracy for EKENE-8.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 4, 'Min-max scaling fitted on the 180 cored rows is applied to both uncored wells. EKENE-7''s highest GR maps to 0.656751 and EKENE-8''s to 1.241800. What does the check show?', '["EKENE-8''s GR was clipped at 1 on 10 rows, so its predictions on those rows are held at the cored maximum", "EKENE-7 is out of range too, as 0.656751 is below the cored mean of GR in min-max units", "EKENE-8''s highest row sits 1.241800 population SDs above the cored mean, a small offset", "10 of EKENE-8''s 30 rows read more gamma ray than any cored row did, and none of EKENE-7''s"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Min-max maps the cored minimum to 0 and the cored maximum to 1, and nothing is clipped, so a new row above the cored maximum maps above 1: 10 of 30 EKENE-8 rows do, the highest to 1.241800, while EKENE-7 stays inside the range. Inside the range is inside the range, whatever the cored mean. Min-max units are fractions of the cored range, 131.100000 gAPI for GR, and never standard deviations.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 5, 'EKENE-8''s range check flags 10 rows. Only because the field is synthetic, the withheld facies shows kNN at k 5 missing 3 rows of that well. Where do the 3 misses sit?', '["All 3 among the 10 flagged rows, as the check was built to catch them", "2 among the flagged rows and 1 inside the range, split by their GR", "All 3 inside the cored GR range, among the 20 rows the check passed", "None in EKENE-8: the 3 misses belong to EKENE-7, whose GR is normal"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A range check sees only the rows that leave the range: kNN missed 3 rows of EKENE-8, and all 3 of the misses sit inside the GR range. Every row of EKENE-8 carries the same 30 gAPI offset, flagged or not. kNN scores EKENE-7 at 1.000000 on the withheld facies, so it misses none there.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 6, 'kNN''s 3 misses on EKENE-8 are all sandstone predicted as shaly-sand. Which stated figures explain them?', '["Sandstone GR has a stated mean of 45 gAPI, shaly-sand 66, and the tool adds 30", "Shaly-sand''s silhouette, 0.152252, the lowest", "Sandstone''s stated NPHI mean is 0.2 against 0.235, and the tool reads NPHI high too", "A held-out score of 0.833333 on EKENE-6"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The generator states the sandstone GR mean as 45 gAPI and the shaly-sand mean as 66 gAPI; 30 gAPI more puts a sandstone''s mean GR above the shaly-sand mean, so its nearest cored rows can be shaly-sand rows. The planted fault is on GR alone. A silhouette describes the cored groups and names no mechanism for these rows, and a held-out score on another well explains no particular miss.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 7, 'With the stated 30 gAPI taken off every EKENE-8 GR value, kNN at k 5 scores 1.000000 against the withheld facies. What does the course say about that correction?', '["The engine''s own repair, applied when min-max flags a whole well above 1, so a caller need not correct GR", "It is possible only because the offset was planted and stated; a real field needs a gamma ray normalisation between wells", "Any well flagged by the range check should lose 30 gAPI, whatever its tool or its logging company", "Proof the tree was worse on EKENE-8, as the tree was not corrected and still reads 0.933333 on that well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In a real field the offset is unknown, and the fix is a gamma ray normalisation between wells, which is the data quality course''s, done before any facies is predicted. The engine builds no gamma ray normalisation and repairs nothing. A flag says rows left the range; it measures no offset, and 30 gAPI is a figure the generator stated for this one well. The tree''s 0.933333 was scored on the uncorrected well, so nothing is compared like for like.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 8, 'Why does the course flag the whole of EKENE-8 for a gamma ray to be normalised, beyond the 10 rows above the cored GR maximum?', '["A well with more than a third of its rows flagged is failed as a whole by the course''s stated rule", "The engine refuses to predict a well once any of its rows maps above 1, so all rows must be flagged", "Flags feed the vote, so flagging 10 rows would alter the other 20", "The offset sits on every row, flagged or not, and the check sees only rows that leave the range"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The whole-well flag is the one that matters, because the planted offset is on every sample and a range check catches only the rows it pushes past the cored maximum; the 3 kNN misses were among the 20 unflagged rows. There is no stated fraction rule. The engine predicts every row it is given and scales nothing away. A flag is written beside the prediction and does not enter any vote.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 9, 'kNN at k 5 and the tree give the same facies on 28 of 30 rows of EKENE-7 and 29 of 30 rows of EKENE-8. How does the course read that?', '["As agreement between two methods trained on the same rows, which is not a check against rock", "Strong support for EKENE-8''s predictions, where the two methods agree on one row more than on EKENE-7", "As a lower bound on accuracy: each well is right on at least as many rows as the two methods agree on", "A reason to average the two predictions, as the engine does above 0.900000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both methods learned from the same 180 cored rows, and whatever is wrong with a new well''s logs reaches both at once. On EKENE-8, counted row by row against the withheld facies, 2 rows are missed by both with the same wrong facies, so agreement there included wrong answers. Agreement bounds nothing about accuracy, and the engine combines no predictions.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 10, 'On EKENE-7, kNN matches the withheld facies on all 30 rows and the tree on 28. What do the 2 rows where the two methods disagree turn out to be?', '["One miss for each method, so the disagreement split the errors evenly", "Rows both methods missed, with each predicting a different wrong facies", "Exactly the tree''s 2 misses, so there a disagreement found every error", "Rows outside the cored GR range, as EKENE-7''s GR maps above 1 on them"]'::jsonb, 2, 'kNN is right on every row of EKENE-7, so any row where the tree differs is a tree miss, and the tree misses exactly 2. No row of EKENE-7 is missed by both methods. EKENE-7''s highest GR maps to 0.656751 on the cored min-max scaler, well inside the range.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 11, 'Which of these belongs in the write-back of a predicted facies for EKENE-8, by the course''s rule?', '["Its predicted facies written into FACIES, so later training runs can use the well", "Accuracy against the withheld facies, 0.900000, as the prediction''s expected accuracy", "Agreement with the tree, 29 of 30 rows, as a measure of how far to trust the facies", "A new channel, FACIES_PRED, beside the empty core facies, which stays null"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course''s table: "a new channel, FACIES_PRED, beside the empty core facies; the core facies stays null". Writing a prediction into the core channel erases the one certain fact, that nobody cored the well, and a later training run would learn from predictions as if they were rock. The withheld facies exists only because the field is synthetic and never enters the write-up, and agreement between methods is not evidence about rock.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 12, 'What does the write-back state as the prediction''s expected agreement, and why that figure?', '["1.000000, kNN''s accuracy on EKENE-7''s withheld facies, the best figure the course measured for this method", "0.833333, kNN at k 5 on EKENE-6 held out and trained on the other five cored wells, the only honest score it carries", "0.988889, the tree''s training accuracy on all 180 cored rows, since the tree was grown on the same rows", "0.866667, kNN at k 3 on EKENE-6, the highest held-out score reached at any k tried on that well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The expected agreement is the held-out score, 0.833333 on EKENE-6, named as a score on one held-out cored well; it is an expectation for wells like the training wells. The withheld facies never enters the write-up. A training accuracy describes the rows a model was grown on. The method written back is kNN at k 5, so a k 3 score belongs to a different model.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 13, 'Which item stands in the write-back as "what is not claimed"?', '["No method, since the channel name already carries it", "No range flags, which belong in a separate QC report", "No core facies and no accuracy for an uncored well", "No training wells, as they are listed in the core log"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course''s table: "what is not claimed | no core facies and no accuracy for an uncored well". The method, the training wells and the range flags are items the write-back carries in full, beside the held-out score; the last line of the table keeps the note from claiming what no uncored well can give.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 14, 'The course writes back the kNN prediction and not the tree''s. On what evidence?', '["kNN at k 5 scored 0.833333 on EKENE-6, held out, against the tree''s 0.766667 at its default depth", "kNN matched EKENE-7''s withheld facies on all 30 rows, where the tree matched only 28 of them", "kNN agreed with itself on more rows of EKENE-8 than the tree did when the two were compared", "The tree splits on CALI, the caliper, which a write-back may never carry in its list of logs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The held-out cored well is the evidence that counts: kNN at k 5 scored 0.833333 on EKENE-6, and the tree at the default depth, grown without EKENE-6, scored 0.766667. The withheld facies exists only in this synthetic field, so a choice resting on it could never be made in a real one. Agreement is between the two methods only, and the tree never splits on CALI; its importance is 0.000000.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-predicting-uncored-wells', 15, 'After the Associate tier, EKENE-7''s rows carried cluster numbers from `assignClusters`; now they carry predicted facies. What is the difference?', '["None in substance: both are the facies of the nearest centre, printed once as a number and once as a name", "A cluster number comes from the core facies of EKENE-7''s own rows, while a predicted facies comes from its logs alone", "A predicted facies is certain once two methods agree, whereas a cluster number is only an estimate", "A predicted facies is learned from core through training rows; a cluster number has no rock behind it until matched"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A cluster is a group the engine made without core, numbered from 0, and the number is a name. A facies predicted by kNN or a tree comes from core through the training rows, and it is called predicted. EKENE-7 has no core facies at all. kNN keeps no centres, and agreement between two methods is not a check against rock.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 1, 'Six rows are passed to `kmeans`. Where does the boundary on k sit, by the course''s table?', '["k 2 to 5 are accepted; 1 and 6 are refused as trivial", "Both 1 and 6 pass; k 0 and k 7 are refused", "Any k from 0 to 6 is accepted; 0 returns one cluster", "k 1 to 5 are accepted; k equal to the rows is refused"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The boundary table: kmeans k, "1 and n accepted (6 rows, k 6)", "0 and n + 1 refused". A single cluster and one cluster per row are both valid calls; the refusal for k 0 reads "k must be a whole number from 1 to 180 (the number of rows)" on the cored rows. The silhouette is the function whose boundary excludes 1 and n, and that rule belongs to it alone.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 2, 'Two cored rows are each passed twice to `kmeans` with k 3. What does the engine say, and what does it count?', '["Three clusters come back, one of them empty, with a warning that a centre could not move from its row", "It refuses naming `k`, since each of the 3 clusters would need at least two of the rows passed to it", "Two clusters come back in place of the 3 asked for, and the basis block records the change of k", "It refuses: 2 distinct rows after scaling, fewer than k = 3, so k-means++ cannot place 3 distinct centres"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s words: "X has 2 distinct rows after scaling, fewer than k = 3: k-means++ cannot place 3 distinct centres". The boundary counts distinct rows, after scaling: distinct rows equal to k are accepted, fewer are refused. Four rows are more than 3, so the rows limit is met; the distinct count is what fails. The engine never changes k or returns an empty cluster.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 3, 'For the silhouette, which numbers of distinct clusters does the boundary accept on n rows?', '["From 1 to n; one cluster scores 0 on every row", "From 2 to n; n clusters score 0 on every row", "From 2 to n - 1; both 1 and n are refused", "From 2 to 10000, the most rows it scores in full"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The boundary table: silhouette distinct clusters, "2 and n - 1 accepted", "1 and n refused". The silhouette compares each row with the next nearest cluster, so one cluster leaves nothing to compare, and n clusters leave every row alone; the refusal on four rows in four clusters reads "labels must hold from 2 to 3 distinct clusters (found 4)". 10000 is the row cap for scoring in full, a different rule.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 4, '`pca` is passed maxSweeps 0. What comes back, and what happens at maxSweeps 1?', '["0 is refused; 1 is accepted, and a sweep that still rotates brings a warning", "0 is accepted and read as no limit at all; 1 is refused as too few sweeps to converge", "0 returns the unrotated matrix; 1 returns eigenvalues sorted once", "Both are refused; the smallest accepted setting is the default 50"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The boundary table: pca maxSweeps, "1 accepted", "0 refused", with the engine''s words "maxSweeps must be a whole number, 1 or more". A run whose last allowed sweep still needed a rotation returns its result with a warning, as the golden `pca-warning-both` does at maxSweeps 1. 50 is the default when maxSweeps is left out, never the floor.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 5, 'The golden `kmeans-assignment-tie-lower-centre` runs one pass on rows (0, 0), (0, 1), (10, 0), (10, 1) and (5, 0) from centres (0, 0) and (10, 0), with no scaling. Which labels come back?', '["0, 0, 1, 1, 1", "0, 1, 0, 1, 0", "0, 0, 1, 1, and a refusal for row 4", "0, 0, 1, 1, 0"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Row 4, (5, 0), is equally far from both centres. Squared distances within 1.00e-12 of the smallest, relative, are tied and go to the lower centre, centre 0. The other four rows are each nearest one centre. A tie is settled by the stated rule and never refused.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 6, 'The golden `agglomerative-grid-ties-ward` clusters (0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1), (5, 0) and (6, 0) with Ward linkage and no scaling. The first three merges are all at height 1.000000. In what order are they made?', '["[4, 5] first, as the pair lying farthest from the others, then [0, 1] and [2, 3] after it", "[0, 1], then [2, 3], then [4, 5]: the tied pair with the lowest cluster ids first", "[0, 2], then [1, 3], then [4, 5]: the columns of the square before its rows", "All three at once, recorded as one row of the linkage matrix of height 1.000000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "merges whose heights are within 1e-12 (relative) of the smallest are tied; the tied pair with the lowest cluster ids wins (the smaller id first, then the other)". 2 of the 5 merges met a tie. A linkage matrix holds one merge per row, n - 1 rows in all, so no two merges share a row, and distance from other points does not order tied merges.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 7, 'At k 4, seed 3, 10 starts on the 180 cored rows, starts 3, 5 and 8 all print the inertia 58.289042. Why does start 3 keep the win?', '["Their largest difference from the winner is 0, inside the band, so they tie and the earliest start keeps it", "Start 3 took the fewest passes of the three, 6, and fewer passes break a tie between starts", "Six decimals agree on all three, which the engine reads as equal, and the first printed wins", "Start 3''s starting rows include row 128, which start 9 also draws, so it was drawn twice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis: "lowest inertia over the runs; a run within 1e-12 (relative) of the best so far does not replace it". The course prints the largest difference, 0, before calling them tied; printing alike at six decimals decides nothing. Passes do not break ties: start 5 took 5 passes, fewer than start 3''s 6, and still lost. A row that opens two starts plays no part in the rule.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 8, 'Two figures a report compares print alike at six decimals. When may the report say they tie?', '["Whenever they print alike at six decimals, since the course prints every figure at that precision", "When their difference is below 1.00e-6, the size of the last printed digit at six decimals", "Only where the engine''s own comparison says so, such as a tied-vote count or a difference inside the band", "Whenever both are distances, as distances within the band of 1.00e-12 always print alike"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Six decimals hide differences far larger than a relative band of 1.00e-12, so a pair can agree at that precision and still fall outside the band. The evidence for a tie is the engine''s own comparison: a tied-vote count, a count of tied steps in the merge history, or a printed difference from the winning start that sits inside the band, as the 0 of the tied starts does. An earlier start also keeps the win when a later one is plainly worse, so the win alone shows no tie. A threshold at the last printed digit is a rule the engine does not use.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 9, 'The correlation PCA''s first component on the cored rows weighs GR 0.423323, RHOB -0.489083, NPHI 0.541336 and PEF -0.537169. Which weight does the sign rule make positive, and why?', '["PEF''s, as the log listed last in the column order takes the positive sign in each component", "NPHI''s, the largest in absolute value, with PEF close behind and far outside the 1.00e-9 band", "GR''s, as the first log is always the one made positive, so a component reads the same in every study", "Both NPHI''s and PEF''s, which lie within the band of each other and so share the positive sign between them"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "in each component the first loading whose absolute value is within 1e-9 (relative) of the largest is made positive". NPHI''s 0.541336 is the largest in size, and PEF''s weight, -0.537169, falls short of it in size by far more than a relative band of 1.00e-9, so only NPHI counts as largest. One sign is chosen per component, so two weights of opposite sign cannot both be positive, and nothing rests on column position.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 10, 'The golden `pca-two-features-sign-tie` has five rows, (1, 2), (2, 3.5), (3, 3), (4, 6.5), (5, 5). Its first component is (0.707107, 0.707107). What makes the first weight positive?', '["The first feature has the larger variance of the two, and the sign follows the feature with the larger spread", "A seeded draw from mulberry32 picks which weight to make positive whenever two weights tie in their size", "The engine refuses to sign such a component and returns it as the solver gave it, with a warning attached", "The two weights are equal in size, both count as largest inside the 1.00e-9 band, and the first is made positive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With two standardised features the first component''s two weights are equal in size, so which is largest would otherwise be decided by rounding. A weight within 1.00e-9 of the largest, relative, inclusive, counts as largest, and the first such weight is made positive. On a correlation matrix both features have variance 1, and the sign rule draws nothing and warns about nothing.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 11, 'Four rows, (1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1) and (0, -1), give covariance eigenvalues 0.666667 and 0.666667. What does `pca` return?', '["The result, with a warning that the directions are not unique and the loadings are one valid choice", "A refusal naming `X`, since two equal eigenvalues leave the components undefined for any eigen solver", "The result with no warning, since the sign rule already fixes both directions of the plane", "A refusal naming `matrix`, as a covariance matrix with a repeated eigenvalue cannot be built"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the engine''s words: "eigenvalues 1 and 2 differ by at most 1e-10 times the largest eigenvalue, so the directions of those components are not unique: the loadings shown are one valid choice". A repeated eigenvalue is a warning; the result comes back. The sign rule fixes a sign, and in a plane of equal spread any direction is as good as another, so no sign rule can make the pair unique.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 12, 'Which test does the engine apply before it warns of a repeated eigenvalue?', '["abs(lambda_k - lambda_(k+1)) < 1.00e-10 x lambda_k, relative to the larger eigenvalue of the pair itself", "Any two eigenvalues, adjacent or not, whose ratio lies within 1.00e-10 of 1, exclusive of it", "abs(lambda_k - lambda_(k+1)) <= 1.00e-10 x lambda_1 for adjacent sorted eigenvalues, inclusive", "The same form with 1.00e-12 in place of 1.00e-10, since that is the band the engine uses for every tie"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The repeated test compares each eigenvalue with the next one in the sorted list and flags the pair when abs(lambda_k - lambda_(k+1)) <= 1.00e-10 x lambda_1, the largest eigenvalue: inclusive, relative to the largest, never to the pair. The boundary table shows a gap of 0.99 of that band flagged and 1.01 not. 1.00e-12 is the band for distances and merge heights, one rule among several, and no band is global.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 13, 'With maxSweeps 1 and a repeated eigenvalue, the golden `pca-warning-both` raises both pca warnings. How does the engine report them?', '["Only the repeated-eigenvalue warning is kept, as it is the more serious", "The call is refused, since two warnings together amount to a failure", "Both are kept, the repeated-eigenvalue warning first, one per result field", "Both are kept, the non-convergence warning first, joined by \"; \""]'::jsonb, 3, 'When both apply the engine keeps both, the non-convergence warning first, joined by "; ", and returns the result: "Jacobi did not converge in 1 sweep (the last sweep still rotated): ...; eigenvalues 1 and 2, 3 and 4 differ by at most 1e-10 times the largest eigenvalue, ...". Warnings never add up to a refusal, and neither one is dropped.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 14, 'With one start per k and seed 265, the elbow over k 1 to 8 reads an inertia of 45.270437 at k 8 against 45.153181 at k 7. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal on kMax, since a rising inertia means k 8 is above what the rows can hold", "Every row of the elbow, k 8 listed in `inertiaRises`, and a warning to raise nInit", "The elbow up to k 7 only, with k 8 dropped and a note that its run failed to converge", "Every row, with k 8''s inertia replaced by k 7''s so the curve keeps falling"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns the figures and warns, in its own words: "inertia rises at k = 8: those runs stopped in a local minimum; raise nInit". A rise is a result with a warning, never a refusal, and no figure is dropped or overwritten. With the default 10 starts the same seed shows no rise, k 8 at 41.697291.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-boundaries-and-caps', 15, 'A k-means call at seed 3, k 4 and maxIter 2 on the cored rows returns `converged` false, `iterations` 2 and inertia 58.432713. Is it a refusal?', '["No: it is a result with a warning, the labels coming from one more pass against the last centres", "Yes: maxIter below the number of passes needed is refused, naming `maxIter` as its field", "No: it is a result with no warning, since `converged` false already carries the whole message", "Yes: the engine refuses any start that has not converged and asks the caller to allow it more passes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s warning: "did not converge in 2 assignment passes: the labels printed come from one more pass against the last centres". A run stopped at maxIter is a result, never a refusal; the only maxIter refusal is for a value below 1. The labels still come back, one per row.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 1, 'The engine scales logs for clustering with one standard deviation and builds the correlation PCA with another. Which pairing is right?', '["Clustering: the sample SD, divisor n - 1; the correlation PCA: the population SD, divisor n", "Both use the population SD, divisor n, the machine learning engine''s scaler throughout", "Clustering: the population SD, divisor n; the correlation PCA: the sample SD, divisor n - 1", "Both use the sample SD, divisor n - 1, so a PCA score and a scaled log compare directly"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conventions table: clustering scaler "standard, population SD (n), fitted on the rows clustered"; PCA matrix "correlation, sample SD (n - 1)", chosen so that each score''s variance equals its eigenvalue and the eigenvalues sum to the number of logs, 4.000000 on the cored rows. On those 180 rows the sample SD is larger by the factor 1.002789 on every log, so a figure quoted without its divisor cannot be reproduced exactly.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 2, 'On the cored rows the covariance PCA''s first component carries 0.998581 of the variance with a GR weight of 0.999905, and the correlation PCA''s first carries 0.682351. What does the pair show?', '["The choice of matrix changes what the figure means: the covariance form is almost a gamma ray PCA", "The correlation form has lost part of the variance to rounding, which the covariance form keeps in full", "GR carries the facies signal, as the covariance form confirms and the correlation form hides", "The two forms disagree about the data, so one of them must be discarded before a study goes any further"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the covariance form GR''s variance in gAPI squared dwarfs the others'', so the first component is almost GR alone; the correlation form lets every log count equally and draws on all four. Neither pair disagrees about the data; each answers a different question, and the choice says which. No variance is lost: a correlation PCA''s eigenvalues sum to the number of logs. A unit''s size is no evidence of facies signal.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 3, 'The engine''s k-means++ takes one candidate per step from one mulberry32(seed) stream. What is the common alternative, and the engine''s stated reason for its choice?', '["Random starting rows with no weighting at all; the engine''s choice is faster on large fields of many wells", "Greedy k-means++ trying several candidates (scikit-learn); one canonical generator lets a learner follow each draw", "Starting from the facies means of the core; the engine''s choice needs no core to begin with", "Greedy k-means++ trying several candidates; the engine''s choice always finds the lowest inertia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conventions table: k-means seeding, this engine "k-means++, one candidate per step, mulberry32(seed)", alternative "greedy k-means++ (scikit-learn tries several candidates)", reason "one canonical generator across the platform; a learner can follow each draw". No seeding guarantees the lowest inertia: at seed 5 ten starts stop at 58.297079, above 58.289042. Speed is not the stated reason.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 4, 'Why does the engine run 10 k-means starts by default, and what does the course say more starts can and cannot do?', '["A stable silhouette needs at least ten starts, and any number fewer than ten is refused by name as `nInit`", "On these rows ten starts always reach the lowest inertia, so the seed stops mattering once it is used", "Averaging the centres of all ten starts into a single set removes the effect of any poor start entirely", "One start can stop in a poor arrangement; more starts make that less likely and do not rule it out"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Across the ten seeds in the course''s table, one start leaves 8 of 10 above 58.289042, and ten starts bring 9 of 10 to it; seed 5 still stops at 58.297079. So a result is quoted with its seed and its number of starts. nInit 1 is accepted, and the lowest-inertia start wins outright; no centres are averaged.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 5, 'The engine counts k-means passes as assignment passes, the confirming pass included. What does that choice match?', '["Centre updates, one fewer than the passes", "scikit-learn''s n_iter_ when the stop is by labels", "The number of starts, 10 by default, times the passes", "Its maxIter setting of 300, which every run reaches"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conventions table: k-means passes, this engine "counted as assignment passes, the confirming pass included", alternative "centre updates", reason "matches scikit-learn n_iter_ when the stop is by labels". Starts are counted separately in `runs`, and maxIter 300 is a ceiling; the teaching clustering''s winning start took 6 passes.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 6, 'The teaching clusters score a mean silhouette of 0.545063 on the scaled logs and 0.443238 on the raw logs. Why does the engine default to the scaled logs?', '["Raw figures are refused outright whenever GR is present, since gAPI dominates the distance", "Higher is better, and the engine reports the more favourable of the two figures", "It scores the space the clusters were made in, so the silhouette is quoted with its scaling", "Once k-means has scaled them, raw logs cannot be scored at all, so no choice is left to make"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conventions table: silhouette distance, this engine "Euclidean on the scaled logs", alternative "raw logs", reason "scores the space the clusters were made in". `scale: ''none''` is accepted and returned 0.443238, so raw scoring is possible and unrefused. A silhouette is measured in a space, and the choice of space is stated, whichever figure is higher.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 7, 'Which description of the engine''s agglomerative output is its stated convention?', '["A scipy linkage matrix, with Ward heights as sqrt(2 x the rise in the sum of squares), so the tree can be re-cut and drawn", "scikit-learn''s children_ array with no heights, the lighter form, since a cut at k needs only the merge order", "A list of cluster labels at every k from 1 to n, one per cut, with no record of the merges that made them", "A dendrogram image of the Ward tree with heights read off its axis, as the one form a reader can follow"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The conventions table: agglomerative ids and heights, this engine "scipy linkage matrix; Ward height sqrt(2 x rise in sum of squares)", alternative "scikit-learn children_ with no heights", reason "the whole tree can be re-cut and drawn". Each row is [smaller id, larger id, height, size], and `cutTree` re-cuts it at any k. The engine returns data, never an image.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 8, 'One-to-one matching of clusters to core facies takes the assignment that matches the most rows, first mapping on ties. What is the alternative, and why did the engine decline it?', '["Majority matching; it lets two clusters share one facies, which one-to-one matching forbids by design", "Random matching with a stated seed; it would change with the seed while the optimum never does", "Matching by centre distance; it needs log units, which the contingency table of counts does not hold", "Greedy matching, cluster by cluster; the engine''s optimum is unique in count and stated in order"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conventions table: one-to-one matching, this engine "maximum rows matched, first mapping on ties", alternative "greedy, cluster by cluster", reason "the optimum is unique in count and stated in order". Majority matching is a second mode the engine also offers, for more clusters than facies; for one-to-one the table names greedy matching as the alternative.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 9, 'Two labellings each put every row in one cluster. What does `adjustedRandIndex` return, and why?', '["0: two groupings of one cluster each carry no information, so no agreement is scored", "A refusal naming `a`, since a single cluster cannot be compared with anything at all", "1: the formula divides zero by zero, and the engine returns 1 to match scikit-learn", "One half, which is the value the engine assigns whenever the index is undefined"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conventions table: ARI of two labellings that each put every row in one cluster, this engine "1", alternative "no value (the formula divides zero by zero)", reason "matches scikit-learn"; the golden `ari-both-one-cluster` returns 1.000000. The index is 1 when two labellings are the same grouping, and these are. The refusals on `a` concern fewer than 2 labels.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 10, 'kNN at k 5 gives a row 4 votes for shaly-sand and 1 for sandstone. A report writes "shaly-sand, confidence 0.8". What has the report done?', '["Quoted the engine''s own probability field, which kNN returns beside every prediction it makes", "Added a claim the engine did not make: it returns votes as counts, and no probability for a predicted facies", "Rounded the engine''s own confidence figure, which is printed at six decimals in the basis block of each result", "Correctly converted a tie-free vote, as the engine states that votes divide by k into a probability"]'::jsonb, 1, 'What is not built: "no probability for a predicted facies". kNN returns its votes as counts, and a tree the counts on each leaf; neither is turned into a probability. A report presenting 4 of 5 votes as a confidence figure adds a claim the engine did not make. No probability field or basis sentence of the kind exists.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 11, 'A study asks for a self-organising map of the Ekene logs. What does the course say about it?', '["The engine builds none; a SOM needs its own seeded engine before a course can teach it", "The engine builds one from its k-means centres, seeded as k-means is, under the name kmeans", "The engine builds one, but only on the correlation PCA scores, which the SOM uses as its grid", "The engine offers it as agglomerative with a fourth linkage, ''som'', beside the three it names"]'::jsonb, 0, 'What is not built: "No self-organising map (the Suite''s older facies code had one that drew from an unseeded generator; it needs its own seeded engine before a course can teach it)". The exported names, in full, hold no SOM, and agglomerative accepts ''ward'', ''complete'' or ''average'' only; any other linkage is refused by name.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 12, 'Which of these is not among the engine''s exported names?', '["cutTree", "pcaTransform", "assignClusters", "crossValidate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The exported names, in full: DEFAULTS, adjustedRandIndex, agglomerative, assignClusters, cartFit, cartPredict, cutTree, elbow, kmeans, knnClassify, matchClusters, pca, pcaTransform, silhouette. The engine does not split wells or cross-validate; a split by whole wells and its scores are the machine learning course''s.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 13, 'The cored rows are passed to `pca` with row 40''s RHOB null. What does the engine do?', '["Fills the null with RHOB''s mean over the cored rows, 2.466911, and adds a warning naming the row", "Refuses by name at the first row and column it meets: \"X[40][1] must be a finite number: fill or drop missing values first\"", "Drops row 40 and fits the other 179 rows, recording the dropped row in the result''s basis block", "Refuses naming `X.RHOB`, as a log with a null entry is treated as a log with zero variance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine does not fill a missing value: a null or non-finite entry in X is refused by name, counting rows and columns from 0, so row 40, column 1, RHOB. Filling or dropping it is the caller''s decision, and conditioning logs belongs to the data quality course. The zero-variance refusal names a constant log, a different fault.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 14, 'A facies note for the Ekene field is assembled from all three tiers. Which item stays out of it?', '["The seed and the number of starts beside the k-means inertia of 58.289042", "The held-out well named beside the kNN score of 0.833333 on EKENE-6", "The withheld facies of EKENE-7 and EKENE-8, which exist only because this field is synthetic", "The drop fractions and the best silhouette printed beside the choice of k"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The withheld facies scored the predictions in this course only because the field is synthetic, and a real note has nothing like them. The seed and starts, the held-out well, and the elbow and silhouette readings beside the choice of k are exactly what the note carries, so that a colleague with the same wells and engine can reproduce every figure without asking a question.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 15, 'For a note to be re-runnable, which setting must be written beside a tree''s figures that a reader might not expect?', '["The column order of the logs, because it decides a tied root and the node numbers below it", "The random_state of the tree, because the engine draws its feature order from that seed", "The seed of the tree''s scaler, since the engine standardises every log before splitting", "The number of starts, as the tree keeps the best of 10 trees grown on shuffled copies of the rows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine breaks a split tie by the lower column index, so the order the logs are passed in decides the printed tree where two logs tie, as NPHI and PEF do at the root. The engine takes no random_state and draws no feature order; a tree scales nothing; and cartFit grows one tree. For a sampled silhouette the note would carry the sample size and seed instead.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'With EKENE-6 held out, the first row of EKENE-6 has its nearest training row, row 58 of EKENE-2, at 0.309960 standard units, and its fifth at 0.600367. What would k 1 predict for it?', '["sandstone, since with one neighbour the engine falls back to the facies that sorts first", "shaly-sand, the core facies of training row 58, the single nearest row", "a refusal, because a vote of one neighbour cannot reach the majority the basis asks for", "limestone, the most common facies among the 150 training rows the model was fitted on"]'::jsonb, 1, 'k 1 copies the facies of the single nearest training row, and row 58 is shaly-sand, as all five of this row''s nearest neighbours are. One vote is a majority of one. The sort-first rule is scikit-learn''s tie rule, and no tie arises with one neighbour. The most common training facies would be the prediction of a vote over every training row.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Every k tried on EKENE-6 held out, from 1 to 15, shows a tied-vote count of 0. What does that let a reader conclude?', '["The engine''s vote tie rule changed no prediction on that well at those k", "The four facies are well enough separated that a tied vote cannot happen on any Ekene well", "The engine and scikit-learn would print different predictions on some rows of EKENE-6", "The engine drops tied rows from the accuracy, so a tie would show as a row missing from the 30"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no tied vote, the rule that gives a tie to the nearer neighbour''s facies never applied, so a tool using the sort-first rule would print the same predictions there. Ties do happen on these wells: row 27 of EKENE-8 ties at k 2. A tied row is predicted by the stated rule and scored like any other, so no row goes missing from the 30.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'The engine chose the nearer-neighbour rule for a tied kNN vote. What reason does the course give?', '["It matches scikit-learn exactly, so predictions agree between the two tools on every row", "Every tied vote is removed, so `tiedVotes` always reads 0 on any well", "It falls back toward the single nearest row, which a learner can check by hand", "Speed: it is faster than the sort-first rule on large sets of training rows"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conventions table: kNN tied vote, this engine "the tied facies whose nearest member comes first", alternative "the facies that sorts first (scikit-learn)", reason "it falls back toward the single nearest row, which a learner can check by hand". The rule settles ties and still counts them; on EKENE-8 at k 2 `tiedVotes` reads 1. Its predictions can differ from scikit-learn''s on tied rows.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'In the equidistant-neighbour golden, what would be predicted had the engine taken row 2 (label c) as the second neighbour in place of row 0 (label a)?', '["c, as row 2 would outvote row 1 by lying nearer to the new row than row 0", "a refusal, as two tied rules in one call cannot both be applied", "c, the facies that sorts last, since b and c are tied at one vote each", "Still b: votes b 1 and c 1 tie, and the tie goes to row 1, the nearer neighbour"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Swap in row 2 and the neighbour list reads row 1, label b, then row 2, label c: one vote each. The vote rule hands a tie to the facies whose nearest member comes first, and row 1 sits on the new row, so the prediction stays b while the printed list changes. Both tie rules apply in turn, and neither refuses.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'A new row passed to `knnClassify` carries only one log, while the training rows carry four. What comes back?', '["A prediction from GR alone, padding the three missing logs with 0", "\"X must have 4 columns, as the model was fitted on\"", "A prediction with a warning that three logs were dropped", "\"Xnew must have 4 columns, like X\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'knnClassify refuses by name, field `Xnew`, in the words of the key. "as the model was fitted on" is how `assignClusters` words its column refusal, and pcaTransform and cartPredict use the same form with "the PCA" or "the tree"; kNN keeps no fitted model and compares against X. The engine neither pads nor drops a log.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Held-out EKENE-6 at k 7 and k 9 both score 0.800000. What may a report say about the two?', '["One of the two must be a rounding, as two different k cannot score exactly alike on one well", "They score the same accuracy on the 30 rows of EKENE-6, which says nothing about other wells", "The larger k is to be preferred, since a larger k averages more rows at no cost in accuracy", "Both beat k 15 by a margin that proves the accuracy falls steadily with every rise in k"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The table gives 0.800000 at both k, each on the 30 rows of EKENE-6. Both are the same count of right rows out of 30, so nothing was rounded, and two k can score exactly alike; one held-out well is one draw, and it says nothing about other wells. The accuracy does not fall steadily: k 3 at 0.866667 is above k 1 at 0.833333. A larger k reaches further from the new row, and whether that helps is what the held-out score measures.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Cored row 6 reads GR 59.800000, RHOB 2.415000, NPHI 0.221000 and PEF 1.990000. Traced down the printed five-channel tree, which leaf does it reach?', '["The shaly-sand leaf of 30 rows, past NPHI above 0.123000, GR at or below 95.600000, RHOB above 2.357000 and GR above 55.950000", "The sandstone leaf of 45 rows, past NPHI above 0.123000, GR at or below 95.600000 and RHOB at or below 2.357000", "The shaly-sand leaf of 11 rows, past GR at or below 55.950000 and then RHOB above 2.381000", "The shale leaf of 29 rows, since its GR of 59.800000 sits above the node 9 threshold of 55.950000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'NPHI 0.221000 is above 0.123000, so the row goes right; GR 59.800000 is at or below 95.600000; RHOB 2.415000 is above 2.357000; GR 59.800000 is above 55.950000, which ends at the leaf "class: shaly-sand (n = 30, counts 0/0/0/30)". The engine''s cartPredict gives shaly-sand for this row, its core facies. The shale leaf sits past GR above 95.600000.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'EKENE-1''s first cored row, a sandstone at 6216 ft, has NPHI 0.225000 and PEF 1.660000, with GR 48.900000 and RHOB 2.284000. Which leaf holds it once `cartPredict` runs it through the tree grown on all 180 cored rows with five channels?', '["A leaf of 4 rows, reached past NPHI above 0.259000, so shaly-sand is predicted", "The leaf of 45 rows, counts 0/44/0/1, so sandstone, its core facies", "The leaf of 2 rows past PEF above 2.490000, so shaly-sand", "Past RHOB at or below 2.381000, the leaf of 5 rows, so sandstone again"]'::jsonb, 1, 'NPHI 0.225000 is above 0.123000; GR 48.900000 is at or below 95.600000; RHOB 2.284000 is at or below 2.357000; NPHI 0.225000 is at or below 0.259000; PEF 1.660000 is at or below 2.490000: the sandstone leaf of 45 rows. The engine''s cartPredict gives sandstone. The RHOB split at 2.381000 sits on the other side of the RHOB 2.357000 split, which this row never reaches.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Which rows of the 180 cored rows reach node 3 of the five-channel tree, the RHOB split at 2.357000 with 97 rows and Gini 0.499522?', '["Rows with NPHI at or below 0.123000 and RHOB above 2.357000", "Those sent right at the root and then left at node 2", "Rows with GR above 95.600000, the shale rows, and no others", "All 180 rows, as every node sees each row"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Node 3 is the left child of node 2: past the root''s right side, NPHI above 0.123000, and node 2''s left side, GR at or below 95.600000. Of node 2''s 126 rows, 29 go right to the shale leaf and 97 reach node 3. The limestone rows stop at the root''s left leaf. A node sees only the rows its path lets through.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'A tree of maxDepth 1 on the 180 cored rows has 3 nodes and 2 leaves, with a training accuracy of 0.577778 on those rows. Grown without EKENE-6, the same depth scores 0.000000 on EKENE-6. How should the second figure be read?', '["As a failed call, since an accuracy of 0 is what the engine returns when a held-out well is refused", "As proof that the root split on NPHI is wrong, since a correct split would place some rows right", "As the training accuracy of a tree grown on EKENE-6 itself, printed in the held-out column by error", "As an accuracy on EKENE-6''s 30 rows for a one-split tree: none of those rows is predicted as its core facies"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The depth table prints both columns: 0.577778 on the rows the tree was grown on and 0.000000 on EKENE-6, held out, at maxDepth 1, and 0.000000 at maxDepth 0 too. A refusal returns no accuracy at all. The figure says the one-split tree grown on the other five wells predicts none of EKENE-6''s rows right: it has 2 leaves, so it can predict at most two facies, and neither is the core facies of any of the 30 rows. Deeper trees reach 0.900000 there.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'At maxDepth 0 the tree on the 180 cored rows is one leaf. What does it predict, and why does its training accuracy read 0.300000?', '["limestone, the facies that sorts first, breaking a tie among the four", "shaly-sand, the facies with the lowest silhouette, the hardest to separate", "no facies at all: a single leaf is refused with the field `maxDepth`", "limestone, the most common facies, and 54 of the 180 rows are limestone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A leaf predicts its most common facies, and limestone holds 54 of the 180 cored rows, the most; the sort-first rule settles only a tie, and the counts 54, 50, 29 and 47 do not tie. Every limestone row is right, 54 of 180, 0.300000. maxDepth 0 is accepted; the refusal is for a negative depth.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'A colleague reads NPHI''s importance of 0.444883 in the five-channel tree as proof that facies depend on NPHI more than on any other log. What does the course say?', '["The reading is right: an importance is a property of the rock, measured from the cored rows", "The reading is right for NPHI and wrong for PEF, whose low importance comes from a scaling error", "An importance describes this tree; to ask whether a log matters, grow the tree without it and score a held-out well", "Importances describe the rock only after they are rescaled by the population SD of each log"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An importance describes the logs this tree happened to use, given the rows, the column order, the tie-breaks and the depth. NPHI took the root only because it came before PEF in the column order; with PEF first, PEF takes it. A tree scales nothing. Whether a log matters to the prediction is a different question, answered by a held-out score without it.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'A tree is grown on the four logs in the order PEF, GR, RHOB, NPHI at depth 1. Which log takes the root?', '["PEF, the lower column index of the two logs tied for the root", "NPHI, which wins the root tie on the cored rows in any column order", "GR, the log with the largest unit, as gAPI outweighs the other logs", "Both, since equal decreases are kept together in one root node"]'::jsonb, 0, 'NPHI and PEF each separate exactly the 54 limestone rows, so their decreases are exactly equal, and the tie goes to the lower feature index. With PEF first, PEF has index 0 and NPHI index 3. GR comes first in the order GR, RHOB, NPHI, PEF and still loses the root there, so its best split lowers the Gini by less; a node splits on one log only.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, '`cartFit` is passed four logs with a names list of only two. What does it return?', '["\"names must be an array of 4 feature names, one per column\"", "Nothing is refused: the two named logs are used and the two unnamed ones are dropped from the tree", "A tree in which the engine names the two missing logs itself, in column order, on the printed lines", "\"X must have 4 columns, as the tree was fitted on\""]'::jsonb, 0, 'cartFit checks its names against the columns of X and refuses by name, field `names`, in the words of the key. The engine neither drops a log nor invents a name for one. The column refusal belongs to cartPredict, which compares new rows with the tree it was given.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'How does the engine compare two candidate thresholds on the same log whose splits give the same decrease?', '["The threshold nearer the log''s mean over the node wins, as it balances the two sides", "The lower threshold wins, after the lower feature index has been applied", "The higher threshold wins, as it sends more rows to the left child", "Both are kept and the tree prints the midpoint of the two thresholds"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "equal decreases (compared exactly on the integer counts) go to the lower feature index, then the lower threshold". Within one log the feature index is the same, so the lower threshold settles it. A node keeps one threshold, and neither the mean nor the rows sent left enters the rule.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'The five-channel tree splits node 4 on NPHI at 0.259000 and node 5 on PEF at 2.490000. How did the engine form such a threshold?', '["As the value of one training row, the last one sent left, copied out to six decimals", "The log''s mean over the node''s rows, rounded to the precision the log carries", "A grid point in fixed steps, chosen so the threshold prints in few digits", "As a/2 + b/2 of two consecutive distinct values of that log among the node''s rows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A candidate threshold sits halfway between two neighbouring distinct values of the log in the node, computed as a/2 + b/2; any value between the two would send the same rows each way. A training value is used only when the midpoint rounds to b. Thresholds such as 2.490000 print short because their midpoints happen to; RHOB at node 10 prints with a tail in the tree''s own lines.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, '`cartPredict` is passed the result of `kmeans` in place of a tree. What does it return?', '["The facies of each row''s nearest k-means centre, read as a one-level tree", "A refusal naming `model`: \"model must be the result of cartFit\"", "A refusal naming `X`: \"X must have 4 columns, as the tree was fitted on\"", "The cluster numbers of the k-means result, returned under the name predictions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each prediction function checks that it was given the right kind of model: cartPredict says "model must be the result of cartFit", as assignClusters says "model must be the result of kmeans" and pcaTransform "model must be the result of pca". The column refusal is real but belongs to new rows with the wrong number of logs for a tree. A k-means result is never read as a tree.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'On EKENE-7, which rows does kNN at k 5 get wrong against the withheld facies, and what does that make of the tree''s 2 disagreements with it?', '["Two; and those are the rows where the two methods disagree with each other", "Three sandstone rows read as shaly-sand, as on EKENE-8, with the tree right", "None; so each disagreement is a tree miss, 28 of 30 right for the tree", "Unknown: the course never reads EKENE-7''s withheld facies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only because the field is synthetic, the withheld facies scores kNN 1.000000 on EKENE-7, all 30 rows; any row where the tree gives another facies is then a tree miss, and the tree scores 0.933333, 28 rows. The three sandstone misses belong to EKENE-8, whose GR reads 30 gAPI high. The course reads the withheld facies once, to check both uncored wells.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'Applied unchanged to EKENE-7, the cored-row min-max scaler returns 1.035264 as that well''s largest PEF value. How is that read?', '["EKENE-7 carries a planted PEF fault, as EKENE-8 carries a gamma ray one, stated by the generator of the field", "The scaler clipped EKENE-7''s PEF at 1, and the printed figure is only a rounding of that clipped value", "Nothing: a value above 1 on min-max only happens for GR, so the figure is read as inside the range", "At least one EKENE-7 row reads PEF above the cored maximum of 5.330000 b/e, and nothing was clipped"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Min-max maps the cored maximum, 5.330000 b/e for PEF, to 1 and clips nothing, so a value above 1 is a reading above any cored row. Checked with the engine, EKENE-7''s highest PEF is above the cored maximum. The generator states one planted tool fault, EKENE-8''s gamma ray; a range check runs on every log, GR included, and on EKENE-7 it flags PEF.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'What gamma ray reading does a value of exactly 1 correspond to on the min-max scaler fitted on the cored rows?', '["56.871111 gAPI, the mean GR of the cored rows, plus one SD", "131.100000 gAPI, the GR range of the cored rows, as a level", "10.100000 gAPI, the lowest GR, as 1 marks the far end", "141.200000 gAPI, the highest GR among the 180 cored rows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Min-max maps each log''s cored minimum, 10.100000 gAPI, to 0 and its maximum, 141.200000 gAPI, to 1; 131.100000 is the range it divides by, which is no reading at all. Standard scaling, with a mean and a population SD, is the other scaler. The write-back flags EKENE-8''s rows above the cored GR maximum of 141.200000 gAPI.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'On EKENE-8, kNN at k 5 misses 3 rows against the withheld facies and the two methods disagree on 1 row. What follows?', '["The tree must miss exactly 1 row of EKENE-8, the row where the methods disagree", "At least 2 of kNN''s misses are rows where the tree gives the same wrong facies", "kNN''s 3 misses are all flagged by the range check, as they sit above the GR range", "The disagreement row is kNN''s only true miss"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Of kNN''s 3 misses, at most 1 can be a row where the methods disagree, so at least 2 are rows where they agree on a wrong facies; counted row by row, 2 of 30 rows are missed by both. The tree scores 28 rows right, missing 2. All 3 kNN misses sit inside the GR range, where the check passes them.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'A predicted facies channel is written back with its method, training wells and range flags, but without its held-out score. What does the course say will happen?', '["Nothing, since any reader can recompute the score", "The engine refuses to write a channel named FACIES_PRED until a score is attached to it", "It will be read later as core: the held-out score is one of the four things it needs", "It will be read as a cluster, since only a score turns a cluster into a predicted facies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course''s rule: "a predicted facies written back without its method, its training wells, its held-out score and its range flags will be read later as core". The engine writes no channel; the write-back is the caller''s. A predicted facies comes from core through kNN or a tree and is no cluster whatever is written beside it.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Why does the course read the withheld facies of EKENE-7 and EKENE-8 only once, and keep it out of every write-up?', '["Because it holds the capstone''s answers, the course hides it from any later step of the Expert tier", "Stored at a lower precision than the cored facies, its accuracy figures are unreliable", "A real uncored well has no such record; it exists only because the generator drew the synthetic field", "It came from a different logging run of the two wells, so it cannot be scaled with the cored rows"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The generator kept the facies it drew for the uncored wells apart from the rows as `withheld`; a real field gives no such check, so the honest statement about an uncored well stops at a held-out score and the range flags. A facies is a name, with no precision or scaling, and nothing in the withheld record belongs to any capstone.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, '`agglomerative` is passed 3001 rows. What does the engine suggest in its refusal?', '["Pass a seed and a sampleSize, which lets it cluster a seeded sample of rows in place of the full set", "Cluster a sample or use kmeans, since it holds every pairwise distance, n(n - 1)/2 of them", "Switch to average linkage, which needs no pairwise distances and so has no cap on the rows", "Split the rows into batches of 3000 and cluster each batch with the same linkage in turn"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s words: "X has 3001 rows, above the 3000 agglomerative clustering accepts (it holds every pairwise distance, n(n - 1)/2 of them): cluster a sample or use kmeans". sampleSize and seed belong to the silhouette. Every linkage starts from pairwise distances, and batching is kNN''s remedy for its pair cap.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, '`silhouette` is passed a sampleSize of 181 on the 180 cored rows. What comes back?', '["The full silhouette of all 180 rows, as a sample that large is read as none", "A seeded sample of 180, with a warning that the size was cut to the rows", "A refusal: \"sampleSize must be a whole number from 2 to 180\"", "A refusal naming `seed`, as a sample above the rows needs a new seed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The boundary table: sampleSize from 2 to min(n, 10000), and n + 1 refused, naming `sampleSize`. The engine does not trim a setting or treat it as absent. The seed refusal is for a sample asked for with no seed.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'A silhouette of the teaching clusters is quoted from a 60-row sample. What must the quote carry, and why?', '["Its size and its seed: 60 rows at seed 3 score 0.521590 and at seed 4 0.525011", "Nothing extra: every sample of 60 rows gives the full figure, 0.545063, to six decimals", "Its scaling only, since the sample is drawn in sorted row order and the seed plays no part", "The number of starts, as the sample is re-drawn ten times and the highest figure is kept"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis: "the first 60 rows of a mulberry32(3) Fisher-Yates shuffle, scored among themselves (scikit-learn sample_size)". Each seed draws its own rows and gives its own figure, and neither reproduces the full 0.545063, so a sampled silhouette is an estimate quoted with its size and seed. Starts belong to k-means.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Which of the engine''s DEFAULTS sets how close a PCA weight must be to the largest to count as largest in the sign rule?', '["`TIE_REL`, 1.00e-12", "`REPEATED_EIGEN_REL`, 1.00e-10", "`SIGN_TIE_REL`, 1.00e-9", "`JACOBI_MAX_SWEEPS`, 50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The DEFAULTS table: `SIGN_TIE_REL` 1.00e-9, "how close a loading must be to the largest to count as largest, in the sign rule". `TIE_REL` is the distance and merge-height band, `REPEATED_EIGEN_REL` the repeated eigenvalue test relative to the largest eigenvalue, and `JACOBI_MAX_SWEEPS` the most sweeps pca takes by default. Three bands, three rules.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'Which DEFAULTS entry caps the distinct clusters or facies that `matchClusters` takes, and at what?', '["`SILHOUETTE_MAX_ROWS`, 10000", "`KMEANS_N_INIT`, 10", "`MATCH_MAX_LABELS`, 50", "`AGGLOMERATIVE_MAX_ROWS`, 3000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The DEFAULTS table: `MATCH_MAX_LABELS` 50, "the most distinct clusters or facies matchClusters takes". `SILHOUETTE_MAX_ROWS` 10000 is the most rows the silhouette scores in full, `AGGLOMERATIVE_MAX_ROWS` 3000 the most rows agglomerative clustering accepts, and `KMEANS_N_INIT` 10 the starts.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'The correlation PCA of the cored rows took 6 Jacobi sweeps and converged. Checked with the engine, what does the same call return with maxSweeps 3?', '["A refusal naming `maxSweeps`, since 3 sweeps are fewer than the 6 the rows are known to need", "The converged result, as the engine runs on to 6 sweeps whenever a smaller limit falls short", "The result, `converged` false, and the warning \"Jacobi did not converge in 3 sweeps (the last sweep still rotated): ...\"", "The result with no warning, since the warning is raised only for a repeated eigenvalue"]'::jsonb, 2, 'maxSweeps 3 is accepted: the only refusal is below 1. Sweep 3 still needed a rotation, so the engine returns the eigenvalues and components after sweep 3, sets `converged` false and warns: "Jacobi did not converge in 3 sweeps (the last sweep still rotated): the eigenvalues and components shown are those after sweep 3". A limit is kept as given, and pca has two warnings.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Does the correlation PCA of the 180 cored rows, eigenvalues 2.729404, 1.101519, 0.097784 and 0.071294, raise the repeated-eigenvalue warning?', '["No: no adjacent pair comes within 1.00e-10 x 2.729404 of each other", "Yes: the last two eigenvalues are both far below the first two and count as repeated", "Yes: any two eigenvalues below 1 are flagged, whatever the gap between them", "No: the warning is raised only on a covariance matrix, never a correlation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The test compares adjacent sorted eigenvalues and flags a pair when abs(lambda_k - lambda_(k+1)) <= 1.00e-10 x lambda_1, the largest, 2.729404 here. Every gap here is far wider than that, and the course states that no pair is repeated on these rows. Nothing depends on how small an eigenvalue is, and the test applies to either matrix: the four-row golden uses a covariance matrix.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'With 10 starts and maxIter 5 at k 4 and seed 3, 6 of the 10 starts converge and the winning start, start 3, does not. What does the result carry?', '["`converged` false and a warning, both describing the winning start; each start''s own flag is in `runs`", "`converged` true, since a majority of the starts converged, and no warning", "A refusal naming `maxIter`, since the winning start did not converge within it", "The best converged start in place of start 3, with a note of the swap"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Six starts converged, but the result reports the start that won on inertia, start 3, which stopped at the limit; the other starts'' own flags sit in `runs`. Starts are never polled and a converged start is never swapped in for the winner. A stop at maxIter returns labels, one per row, and is never refused.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'An elbow run finds two values of k with exactly the same highest mean silhouette. Which does `bestSilhouetteK` report?', '["The larger k, as more clusters fit the rows more closely", "The smaller k, by the engine''s stated tie rule", "Neither, as the engine picks no k and prints only the table", "The k whose inertia drop fraction is larger of the two"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis: "bestSilhouetteK has the highest mean silhouette; a tie goes to the smaller k. No elbow is picked automatically: read the drops". The engine does report a best silhouette k; what it never picks is an elbow. The drop fractions are printed for the reader and take no part in the tie.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, '`cutTree` is passed a two-merge linkage matrix over three rows of data, whose row 1 names a cluster id that no earlier merge has made. What is refused?', '["Nothing: cutTree creates the missing cluster at height 0 and cuts the matrix it has", "The whole matrix, as empty: \"linkageMatrix must be the non-empty linkageMatrix of agglomerative\"", "The row, as a repeat: \"linkageMatrix[1] merges id 0, which linkageMatrix[0] already merged\"", "The row, by name: \"linkageMatrix[1] must be [id1, id2, height, size] with whole ids 0 <= id1 < id2 < 4\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'cutTree checks that a passed matrix describes a real tree. An id not yet made fails the form check on that row, in the words of the key. The empty-matrix refusal and the merged-twice refusal are real messages for other faults: no rows at all, and an id used by two merges. Nothing is created to repair a matrix.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, '`kmeans` is passed starting centres through init together with nInit 5. What happens?', '["Five starts, each one beginning from the given centres, with the best of the five kept", "The given centres are used for one start and the other four starts use k-means++ seeding", "A warning that nInit was ignored, then a single start from the centres", "A refusal: \"nInit must be 1 (or left out) when init gives the starting centres\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Given centres fix the start completely, so more starts would repeat it; the engine refuses, naming `nInit`, in the words of the key. It neither mixes seeding methods nor ignores a setting with a warning.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'For the clustering scaler, the engine uses standard scaling on the population SD. What alternative does the course name, and why was it declined?', '["The sample SD, n - 1; it gives larger scales and so shorter distances between rows", "Scaling each well on its own statistics; it would hide the gamma ray fault in EKENE-8", "No scaling or min-max; logs in different units would otherwise be ruled by GR", "A robust scale from the median; the engine prefers it only when a log has outliers"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conventions table: clustering scaler, this engine "standard, population SD (n), fitted on the rows clustered", alternative "no scaling, or min-max", reason "logs in different units would otherwise be ruled by GR". Scaling each well on its own statistics is a wrong way the course measured on kNN, and the table names no such alternative; median scaling is not built, and outliers belong to the data quality course.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Why does the engine fix the sign of every PCA component by a stated rule?', '["So the same data give the same signs; the alternative is whatever the solver returns", "To make the first component always load GR positive, as the gamma ray is the first log passed", "Signs let the loadings be read as rock types directly, without matching them against core", "Eigenvalues must come out positive, and a negative sign would flip a variance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The conventions table: PCA sign, this engine "largest absolute weight positive, with a band", alternative "whatever the solver returns", reason "the same data give the same signs". On the cored rows the first component''s largest weight is NPHI''s. A direction and its negative are the same direction, eigenvalues are variances whatever the sign, and rock types are read by matching against core.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'A study wants some rows left out of every cluster as noise. What does the course say the engine offers?', '["Agglomerative clustering labels as noise the rows it merges at the greatest heights", "k-means marks as noise the rows whose distance to their centre is the largest of all", "A noise option in silhouette that drops every row whose score falls below 0", "Nothing of the kind: density clustering is not built, so every row is placed in a cluster"]'::jsonb, 3, 'What is not built lists no density clustering (DBSCAN) and no spectral clustering; clustering is k-means and agglomerative, and each places every row. The silhouette scores rows, and the 3 below 0 in the teaching clusters stay in their clusters. Neither k-means nor agglomerative labels a row as noise.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Which course owns scoring every cored well in turn, and the precision and recall of each facies?', '["The machine learning course: this engine does not split wells or cross-validate", "This course, in the Expert tier, through the held-out EKENE-6 score of 0.833333", "The data quality course, which also owns gamma ray normalisation between wells", "The petrophysics course, which owns porosity, saturation and net pay from these same logs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine does not split wells or cross-validate; a split by whole wells and its scores belong to the machine learning course, whose report is read here and never re-derived. This course states one held-out well. The data quality course owns log conditioning and gamma ray normalisation, and petrophysics owns porosity, saturation and net pay.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'A facies note gives accuracies of 0.950000 and 0.833333. Which wording names their rows as the course requires?', '["0.950000 is one-to-one matching on the 180 cored rows; 0.833333 is kNN at k 5 on the 30 rows of EKENE-6, held out", "0.950000 is the k-means accuracy and 0.833333 the kNN accuracy, both on the Ekene field as a whole", "0.950000 is kNN on the 180 cored rows it trained on; 0.833333 is k-means on the uncored wells", "Both are training accuracies on the 180 cored rows, one for the clusters and one for the neighbours"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Accuracy names its rows: the training rows of a tree, a held-out cored well, or, only in this synthetic field, the withheld facies of an uncored well. One-to-one matching of the teaching k-means scores 0.950000 on the 180 cored rows; kNN at k 5, trained on the other five cored wells, scores 0.833333 on EKENE-6. No accuracy is claimed for an uncored well.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'In the facies note, what is written on the line for how k was chosen?', '["Picked by the elbow: k 4, which the engine locates automatically at the largest drop fraction", "Silhouette first: k 3 holds the best figure, 0.690362, the engine''s recommended number", "The core describes 4 facies; the drop fractions and the best silhouette, at k 3, are printed beside the choice", "Lowest inertia: k 4 at 58.289042, the best fit of any k the elbow tried"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The note writes the choice as a reading of printed tables: the core describes 4 facies, and the drop fractions 0.668972, 0.282430 and 0.112303 at k 3, 4 and 5 and the best silhouette at k 3 sit beside it. No elbow is picked automatically, and the silhouette''s best k disagrees with the core''s 4. Inertia always falls as k grows, so the lowest inertia is never the answer.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'The facies note carries a line on where the clustering fails. What does it say?', '["Shaly-sand: 9 of its 47 rows fall with sandstone", "Limestone: its 54 rows split between two clusters", "Shale: its 29 rows fall with shaly-sand at k 4", "Sandstone: 9 of its 50 rows fall with shaly-sand"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The contingency table of the teaching clustering puts 50 sandstone and 9 shaly-sand rows in cluster 1, so matched to sandstone its shaly-sand rows are scored wrong; shaly-sand''s recall is 0.808511. Limestone and shale each fill one cluster alone, 54 and 29 rows, and every sandstone row lands in the sandstone cluster.'),
  ('facies', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Of the three rules that settle a distance or height tie, which is the agglomerative one?', '["Whichever merge would make the cluster holding the most rows wins", "The tied pair of merges with the lowest cluster ids wins", "A merge reached first by k-means++ seeding wins", "Of the tied merges, the one with the higher Ward height wins"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each function breaks a tie by its own stated rule inside the 1.00e-12 band: k-means gives a tied row to the lower centre, kNN takes the lower row, and agglomerative takes the tied pair with the lowest cluster ids, the smaller id first. Tied merges sit within the band of the smallest height, so height cannot order them, and neither size nor any seeding enters.');
